THE SAME DOOR AS BH-0360, BUT THE ROOM BEHIND IT IS EMPTIER. `consumeGreigeStock` (greige-stock.service.ts:451-468) splits on whether the requested quantity fits in the reserved pool:
```ts
if (quantity <= reserved) {
  newReserved = reserved - quantity;        // <- NO check in this branch
} else {
  const fromAvailable = quantity - reserved;
  if (fromAvailable > available) throw new Error('Insufficient stock');   // <- the ONLY check
  ...
}
```
**A negative quantity takes the FIRST branch — which has no check whatsoever.** With `quantity = -500` and a typical `reserved = 0`: `-500 <= 0` is **true**, so `newReserved = 0 - (-500) = **500**`. **Five hundred units of reserved stock, created from nothing** — and `quantityConsumed` is driven negative at the same time. **This is worse than the fabric/lace/thread siblings I found in `challan.service.ts` (280/310/348):** those at least have a weak floor check that a negative input slips past. **Here the negative input doesn't slip past the check — it takes the branch the check isn't in.** REACHABILITY IS THE SAME PROVEN DOOR: `POST /challans/quick-issue` has **no `validateBody`** (BH-0360), spreads the raw body, and `issueChallan()` calls straight into `consumeGreigeStock(item.greigeStockId, qty, ...)` (challan.service.ts:221). **And `greige_stock` is your LARGEST live stock table — 61 rows.** So this is the fourth member of a family, on the most-used table, with the weakest guard of the four. STATUS: **ARMED, NOT FIRED** — the form sends positive numbers. FIX: the single positivity guard inside `createChallan()` recommended in BH-0362 kills all four at once. That is the whole argument for putting invariants in the service rather than the route.
